'Full legal analysis of New York''s $4.6B Polymarket lawsuit: NY Penal Law §225, Executive Law §63(12), CEA preemption, and the circuit split heading to the Supreme Court.',
'On September 24, 2026, Attorney General Letitia James and Governor Kathy Hochul filed what may become the most consequential prediction market case in American history. The complaint names QCX LLC d/b/a Polymarket US and asserts dual claims under New York Polymarket lawsuit laws: NY Executive Law §63(12) for repeated illegality in conducting business and NY Penal Law Article 225 for promoting gambling. The damages sought exceed $4.6 billion, calculated through treble damages on New York-sourced revenue plus $100,000 per sports wager offer.

The core tension is straightforward but legally profound. Polymarket holds a CFTC Designated Contract Market license, acquired through its $112 million purchase of QCEX/QC Clearing LLC and formalized under CFTC Letter No. 25-48 on December 11, 2025. Polymarket argues that the Commodity Exchange Act preempts state gambling laws entirely. New York disagrees. A circuit split between the Third Circuit, the Ninth Circuit, and Judge Analisa Torres in the Southern District of New York has made Supreme Court review near-certain. The outcome will determine whether states retain any authority to regulate CFTC-licensed prediction markets, or whether federal licensing creates an impenetrable shield against 50 separate state regulatory regimes.

## NY Penal Law Article 225 and the "Contest of Chance" Question

NY Penal Law §225.00 defines gambling as "staking something of value on the outcome of a contest of chance or future contingent event not under his control or influence." The critical word is "or." The statute contains two independent prongs. The first, "contest of chance," maps onto traditional gambling analysis. The second, "future contingent event not under one''s control," captures precisely the activity Polymarket facilitates.

This breadth makes New York unusual. Many states define gambling exclusively around contests of chance, which creates room for prediction market operators to argue their contracts are skill-based financial instruments. New York forecloses that argument. A contract on whether a hurricane will make landfall, whether a political candidate will win an election, or whether a sports team will prevail all qualify as "future contingent events not under the user''s control." The statute requires no analysis of skill versus chance.

The age issue compounds Polymarket''s exposure. Polymarket permits users aged 18 to 20 to trade. New York requires users to be at least 21 for mobile sports betting under the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law §1367. If Polymarket''s contracts constitute gambling under Article 225, it has offered those contracts to individuals New York considers legally incapable of consenting to them.

The complaint also includes a federal Wire Act cause of action under 18 U.S.C. §1084, targeting Polymarket''s transmission of wagers across state lines via internet infrastructure. This cause of action operates independently of the state gambling prongs and exposes Polymarket to federal criminal liability alongside the state civil claims.

On a plain text reading of §225.00, the AG''s burden is remarkably low. The AG need not prove Polymarket''s contracts are games of chance. The complaint need only establish that the contracts involve future contingent events not under the user''s control.

## NY Executive Law Section 63(12): A No-Fault Enforcement Weapon

Section 63(12) is the most powerful civil enforcement tool in New York''s regulatory arsenal. Its deployment signals that the AG treats this as an existential enforcement action, not a regulatory negotiation. The [official NY AG press release](https://ag.ny.gov/press-release/2026/attorney-general-james-and-governor-hochul-announce-lawsuit-against-polymarket) outlines the full complaint, relief sought, and the AG''s legal theory in detail.

The statute requires no scienter. The AG need not prove Polymarket intended to violate the law or acted in bad faith. The standard is "repeated or persistent fraud or illegality in the carrying on, conducting or transaction of business." The "capacity or tendency to deceive" standard means the AG does not need to prove actual consumer harm.

The remedial toolkit is sweeping: injunctions, full disgorgement, treble damages, receiver appointment, asset freezes, and investigative subpoenas with court-order force. The AG can seek all simultaneously.

The no-scienter element is particularly devastating for Polymarket. The company cannot defend by arguing good-faith reliance on its CFTC DCM license. Even if Polymarket genuinely believed its federal license immunized it from state law, that belief is irrelevant under §63(12). The question is whether the conduct was illegal, not whether the actor knew it was.

The $4.6 billion figure derives from treble damages on New York-sourced revenue, augmented by $100,000 per sports wager offer. For a platform with Polymarket''s volume, these statutory multipliers produce sums that dwarf the company''s reported U.S. equity. The receiver appointment remedy poses the most existential threat: a court-appointed receiver could seize operational control of Polymarket''s U.S. business.

## CEA Preemption: The Federal Exclusive Jurisdiction Argument

Polymarket''s defense rests on a single structural claim: the Commodity Exchange Act occupies the field and leaves no room for state gambling regulation of contracts listed on a CFTC-designated exchange.

The textual foundation is CEA §2(a)(1)(A), codified at 7 U.S.C. §2(a)(1)(A), which grants the CFTC "exclusive jurisdiction" over "accounts, agreements, and transactions involving swaps or contracts of sale of a commodity for future delivery" traded on designated contract markets. If Polymarket''s event contracts qualify as "swaps," this exclusive jurisdiction clause arguably displaces state authority entirely.

The CEA''s swap definition under §1a(47)(A)(ii) includes any contract "dependent on the occurrence, nonoccurrence, or the extent of the occurrence of an event or contingency associated with a potential financial, economic, or commercial consequence." Polymarket argues its event contracts fit squarely within this language. The "financial, economic, or commercial consequence" requirement, Polymarket contends, is satisfied because the contracts themselves create financial consequences for their holders.

Polymarket''s DCM license, obtained through its $112 million acquisition and formalized under CFTC Letter No. 25-48, provides the institutional predicate. As a designated contract market, Polymarket faces comprehensive federal regulation including capital requirements, reporting obligations, and market surveillance duties.

The CEA §2 savings clause complicates this analysis. The statute provides that nothing in the CEA "shall be deemed to supersede or limit the jurisdiction at any time conferred on other regulatory authorities." New York reads this as express congressional intent to preserve state enforcement authority. Polymarket reads it as limited to other federal agencies.

Conflict preemption offers Polymarket a stronger textual hook. DCMs must provide "impartial nationwide access" under the CEA. If individual states can ban specific contracts or prohibit users from accessing a federally licensed exchange, nationwide access becomes impossible. This is Polymarket''s best doctrinal argument, though it depends on first establishing that event contracts are "swaps" under the CEA.

## The Circuit Split: Third Circuit, Ninth Circuit, and SDNY Torres

Three federal tribunals have addressed the CEA preemption question and reached three incompatible conclusions. Supreme Court review is functionally inevitable.

The Third Circuit fired first. In KalshiEX LLC v. Flaherty, No. 25-1922 (3d Cir. April 6, 2026), a 2-1 panel held that the CEA likely field-preempts state gambling laws as applied to contracts listed on a designated contract market. Judge Porter''s majority reasoned that CFTC exclusive jurisdiction over DCM-listed contracts leaves no regulatory space for state gambling statutes. Judge Roth dissented, arguing that the CEA savings clause and the historical police power over gambling preserved concurrent state authority. [Holland & Knight''s analysis of the Third Circuit KalshiEX ruling](https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/04/federal-appeals-court-cftc-jurisdiction-over-sports-event-contracts) provides the most thorough doctrinal breakdown of the field preemption reasoning and its implications for DCM operators.

The Ninth Circuit reached the opposite result five months later. In KalshiEX LLC v. Assad, No. 25-7516 (9th Cir. August 28, 2026), a unanimous panel held that sports event contracts are not "swaps" under the CEA because sports outcomes lack the "financial, economic, or commercial consequence" required by §1a(47)(A)(ii). No swap status means no CFTC exclusive jurisdiction, which means no preemption. The panel distinguished between the financial consequence of the contract itself and the financial consequence of the underlying event, requiring the latter.

Between these two decisions, Judge Analisa Torres of the Southern District of New York denied Kalshi''s preliminary injunction on July 8, 2026, holding that the CEA §2 savings clause expressly preserves state authority. This ruling carries particular weight because it issued in the same district where Polymarket''s case now sits as Case No. 1:26-cv-08338.

Post-Loper Bright Industries v. Raimondo, 144 S. Ct. 2244 (2024), courts independently interpret statutory terms like "gaming" under CEA §5c(c)(5)(C) without Chevron deference. The CFTC cannot resolve the preemption question through rulemaking alone. New Jersey filed its certiorari petition on September 3, 2026, with Crypto.com and Robinhood joining as supporting parties. The circuit split is clean, the question squarely framed. Certiorari should be granted before the end of the October 2026 Term.

## Polymarket''s Dual-Track Federal Litigation Strategy

Polymarket responded to the September 24 filing with two parallel federal actions, revealing sophisticated litigation planning and a recognition that state court is hostile terrain.

First, Polymarket removed the AG''s complaint to the Southern District of New York under 28 U.S.C. §1441, docketed as Case No. 1:26-cv-08338. The removal rests on the complete preemption doctrine: Polymarket argues that its CEA preemption defense is so fundamental that the state claims are necessarily federal in character.

Second, Polymarket filed an independent declaratory judgment action, docketed as Case No. 1:26-cv-08345-UA, seeking an affirmative ruling that the CEA preempts New York''s gambling statutes as applied to DCM-listed contracts.

The strategic logic is clear. Removal neutralizes the AG''s home-court advantage. The declaratory judgment suit forces the preemption question to the front of the litigation rather than allowing it to be treated as an affirmative defense raised after extensive state-law discovery.

New York will almost certainly move to remand. Complete preemption is a narrow doctrine, and the savings clause lacks obvious support for it under current precedent. If remanded, Polymarket loses its federal forum. If Judge Torres draws the removal case, her July 2026 Kalshi ruling is the most directly applicable precedent in the district, putting Polymarket before a judge who has already rejected the core preemption argument.

## What Comes Next: Supreme Court and Industry Impact

The NJ certiorari petition filed September 3, 2026 will likely be acted on during the Court''s October 2026 conference. If granted, the Polymarket SDNY case will almost certainly be stayed pending resolution.

The industry stakes are enormous. Polymarket is currently valued at more than $20 billion. A ruling against federal preemption would subject every CFTC-licensed prediction market operator to a 50-state patchwork of gambling licensing requirements, each with different age thresholds, product restrictions, and tax obligations. The regulatory arbitrage that prediction markets currently exploit — federal licensing as a substitute for state-by-state compliance — would collapse. As [CNBC reported when both lawsuits were filed](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/09/24/new-york-sues-polymarket-us-two-months-after-filing-suit-against-kalshi-.html), the simultaneous filings suggest months of coordinated preparation on each side.

The 44 state AGs'' coordinated opposition to the CFTC''s pending event contract rulemaking signals that political momentum runs strongly against federal preemption. Whatever the Supreme Court decides, the regulatory environment for prediction markets will look fundamentally different by 2028.

### Can Polymarket continue operating in New York during litigation?

Technically yes, absent emergency injunctive relief. But the AG has sought a preliminary injunction under §63(12). If granted, Polymarket must cease New York operations pending trial. Continued operation also increases damages, as each additional sports wager offer adds $100,000 to the AG''s claim.

### Does a CFTC DCM license shield operators from state gambling laws?

Not under current precedent. The Ninth Circuit and Judge Torres in SDNY both held that a DCM license does not preempt state gambling regulation. Only the Third Circuit found likely preemption. Until the Supreme Court resolves the split, the license''s protective value depends on geography. Operators should conduct state-by-state regulatory exposure analyses now.

### How does Loper Bright affect the preemption analysis?

Loper Bright eliminated Chevron deference. Courts now independently interpret ambiguous terms like "gaming" in CEA §5c(c)(5)(C) rather than deferring to the CFTC. The agency cannot resolve preemption through rulemaking alone. This fundamentally changes the litigation calculus: Polymarket cannot lobby its way to a favorable CFTC rule and rely on judicial deference to sustain it.',
